--TEST--
A statically `null` operand renders as the empty string at every implicit string site, agreeing with the `?string` holding one — `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The row the four refusals beside this case do *not* include. `null` has an
// answer in PHP — the empty string — and Novis already gives that answer for a
// `?string` holding one, which is the same value one type wider: the
// conversion is picked from the tag at run time there and from the static
// type here. What this asserts is that the two **agree**, so that narrowing a
// binding from `?string` to `null` never changes what a line prints.
?string $held = null;
echo "[", null, "]", "[", $held, "]\n";

// The same agreement at the other three sites: `.`, an interpolated piece and
// `.=`.
string $joined = "a" . null;
string $interpolated = "x{$held}y";
string $appended = "b";
$appended .= null;
echo "[", $joined, "][", $interpolated, "][", $appended, "]\n";
--EXPECT--
[][]
[a][xy][b]
